				<div class="leftcol game_description_column"  data-panel="{&quot;flow-children&quot;:&quot;column&quot;}" >

					
												<div id="earlyAccessHeader" class="early_access_header">
		<div class="heading">
												<h2 class="inset">Early Access Game</h2>
					<p class="inset subtitle">Get instant access and start playing; get involved with this game as it develops.</p>
				
				<p><span>Note:</span> Games in Early Access are not complete and may or may not change further. If you are not excited to play this game in its current state, then you
									should wait to see if the game progresses further in development. <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/earlyaccessfaq/?snr=1_5_9_">Learn more</a></p>
					</div>
				<div id="earlyAccessBody" class="devnotes">
							<div class="devs_say">What the developers have to say:</div>
			
			<div id="devnotes_expander">
									<h3>Why Early Access?</h3>
					“Expect bugs and performance issues during Early Access. Bug fixes and performance optimization are ongoing.<br />
<br />
While the game is playable in co-op already with a complete loop, we want to work with the community to make it better. We want to see what becomes exploitable, which enemies stay threatening, and which systems create the funniest or most memorable situations.<br />
<br />
We also want to improve the enemy design by adding more unique scenarios and stalking behavior. The game has an interesting balance between looksmaxxing and how that affects gameplay. We want more time and feedback to find what is right. It has been a lot of work to get things to this state, but we 100% think that a more chaotic extraction based on your looks and tools is going to be the key to making the game more fun. We want the chance to create more unique experiences that really integrate well with our items (like Gua Sha, etc.) and player actions like mewing.<br />
<br />
You can expect us to listen to all player feedback, fixing bugs, tuning things and balancing difficulty, while we make the gameplay feel more fun and rewarding.”
					<h3>Approximately how long will this game be in Early Access?</h3>
					“We're planning for roughly 3 to 6 months in Early Access.”
					<h3>How is the full version planned to differ from the Early Access version?</h3>
					“The core loop is already in place; the goal for the full version is to deepen and polish it rather than replace it. We plan to:<br />
<br />
- Expand the enemy roster and add new hazards, events, and encounters<br />
- Add more items, expand the frathouse dungeon generation, add locations, and things to do between parties<br />
- Deepen the looksmaxxing progression and the consequences of your choices<br />
- Improve enemy AI, balance, and the overall moment-to-moment feel<br />
- Add more audio, UI polish, and accessibility and localization options<br />
- Continue hardening multiplayer stability and performance<br />
- More silly ways to die (traps, etc)<br />
- More animations for using items”
					<h3>What is the current state of the Early Access version?</h3>
					“Fully playable in online co-op today through an escalating quota deadline. <br />
<br />
Currently:<br />
- 64 items + 62 grocery items (126 total)<br />
- 8 looks stats<br />
- 19 peptide side effects<br />
- 4 traps<br />
- 7 deployable upgrades<br />
- 6 levels (2 fully proc-gen)<br />
- 15 enemies<br />
- 82 achievements<br />
<br />
This is a genuine Early Access build: expect bugs, rough edges, placeholder art in places, and systems that will change as we tune them. Please buy Mogged for what it is today: a rough but complete and genuinely playable co-op experience, rather than for what it may become later.”
					<h3>Will the game be priced differently during and after Early Access?</h3>
					“We may raise the price over the course of Early Access as we add content and polish, and again when the game leaves Early Access. Buying during Early Access will always be the cheapest way to get Mogged, and every Early Access owner keeps the full game at no extra cost.”
					<h3>How are you planning on involving the Community in your development process?</h3>
					“We'll be listening to the community through the Steam forums, community hub, and a Discord where players can report bugs, share clips, and suggest ideas. Because Mogged is a co-op game, group feedback on balance and enemy design directly guides what we work on next, and we plan to share progress and reasoning as the game develops so players can see where it's heading.”
							</div> <!-- /devnotes_expander -->
			<a class="morebutton" id="devnotes_more" href="#"  onclick="$J('#devnotes_expander').css({'max-height': 'none'}); $J(this).hide(); return false;">Read more</a>
		</div>
	</div>
										
					
